;;; A keyword pair as a guard's argument reads nothing, in silence —
;;; regression `kwpair-in-guard` (`corpus/regressions.toml`); a keyword
;;; pair is no guard's argument (Q-M1e2.18).
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Four facts of `p`, and one rule whose `eq` guard writes the keyword pair
;;; `:k a` as its second argument. (`neq` is shape-pinned, so `(neq ?x :k a)`
;;; is a parse error; `eq` is a generic list, so this parses.)
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A guard compares two values, and a keyword pair is none. A keyword pair
;;; written as a guard's argument is refused at load, with a line in Ein's
;;; spelling naming the rule and the clause. Every command that loads the
;;; program exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; At `f8c10730` the program loaded, and the guard compared the pair as a raw
;;; IR node, equal to no value, so `e` never fired: root held no `hit-eq`, exit
;;; 0, with no word of the pair (`ein saturate --dump FILE` and `ein solve FILE`
;;; at `f8c10730`, release and debug). Now the loader refuses it: *kb load
;;; error: rule 'e': its :match writes (eq ?x :k a), and the keyword pair :k a
;;; is no value — a guard compares two values, each a variable a premise before
;;; it binds, a name, a string or an integer*.
;;;

(relation p Node) (relation q Node) (relation f Node) (relation hit-eq Node) (relation hit-neq Node)
(p a) (p b) (p 1) (p (f a)) (q a)
(rule e () :match (and (p ?x) (eq ?x :k a)) :assert (hit-eq ?x))
